{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Implementation design finding (verified by probing the installed SDK + facades): the READ facade _reads.py:66 catches ReadError and re-raises a GENERIC RebarError (subclass flattened), so a TicketNotFoundError at reads.py:310/313 does NOT reach the MCP boundary as a subclass — error_code_for(RebarError) would hit the command_failed fallback, breaking the show->ticket_not_found AC. Fix: the read facade attaches error_code='ticket_not_found' to the RebarError it raises when the underlying ReadError isinstance TicketNotFoundError; error_code_for reads that attr. By contrast rebar.claim PRESERVES ConcurrencyError (a RebarError subclass, no error_code, no .message) so isinstance-mapping + str(exc) handles it. Classifier precedence: (ConcurrencyError|ConcurrencyMismatch)->concurrency_conflict; TicketNotFoundError->ticket_not_found; error_code attr; TrackerRootError->tracker_root_unresolved; LLMError->llm_unavailable; else command_failed. This refines the mechanism within the plan's unchanged ACs."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786153017834299001,"uuid":"3b3057d3-e5eb-42d2-a6a9-22fbd82c90c3"}